Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action, 1897-1898 Yukon.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S BACKYARD - DAY

Period live-action. BUCK, a four-year-old, broad-chested St. Bernard-Scotch shepherd dog with long fur, a brown muzzle, and a white blaze down his chest, rules the lawns, orchard, and swimming tank.

(SOUND: splashing water, children laughing, poplar leaves rustling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Because men, groping in Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal, thousands were rushing into the Northland. These men wanted dogs.

Buck carries Judge Miller's grandsons on his back, escorts the daughters through the orchard, then settles at JUDGE MILLER'S feet beside a library fire glimpsed through open doors.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was king--king over all creeping, crawling, flying things of Judge Miller's place, humans included.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

At the little flag station, MANUEL, a worried gardener's helper, leads Buck from the dark orchard toward HARRIS, a hard-faced stranger holding a stout rope.

(SOUND: a distant locomotive whistle and coins chinking)

HARRIS
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Harris slips the rope beneath Buck's collar and takes the loose end. Buck growls; Harris twists. Buck lunges, is thrown onto his back, and struggles as the rope closes his throat.

(SOUND: Buck's choking snarls; the train brakes shriek)

Buck's eyes glaze. Harris and Manuel heave his limp body into the baggage car as the train pulls away.

EXT. SEATTLE DOG-BREAKER'S YARD - DAY

Two days later, Buck lies thirsty and bloodshot in a splintered crate inside a high-walled yard. MURPHY, a stout dog-breaker in a sagging red sweater, studies him with a club and a hatchet.

(SOUND: the hatchet biting crate wood; Buck snarling behind the slats)

Murphy opens a gap. Buck bursts through it in a hundred and forty pounds of fury.

MURPHY
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Murphy's club catches Buck in midair. Buck charges again and again; each blow smashes him into the dirt.

(SOUND: heavy club impacts, Buck's roar turning to a ragged whine)

At last Buck staggers, bleeding from nose and mouth. Murphy brings water and raw meat, then fearlessly pats the battered head.

MURPHY
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well and the goose hang high.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, he knew that; but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

PERRAULT, a little, wiry French-Canadian courier, arrives with CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MURPHY
Three hundred, and a present at that.

Money changes hands. Perrault leads Buck and Curly away.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - MORNING

The steamer NARWHAL pushes through cold gray water. Perrault brings Buck and Curly on deck beside FRANCOIS, a broad, swarthy half-breed driver. Nearby stand SPITZ, a large snow-white husky, and DAVE, a gloomy, lean sled dog.

(SOUND: propeller throb, gulls crying, rigging snapping in the wind)

At feeding, Spitz steals from Buck's ration. Francois's whip reaches Spitz before Buck can.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Perrault and Francois were fair men, calm and impartial in administering justice.

The propeller falls silent. Buck steps onto the white surface of the shore, sinks into snow, sniffs it, and licks it. The snow bites like fire and vanishes on his tongue.

(SOUND: dock ropes creaking; men laughing at Buck's startled snort)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Near the log store at Dyea, Buck watches Curly wag her tail at a wolfish husky.

(SOUND: wind scouring the beach and sled bells jingling)

The husky leaps, tears Curly's face from eye to jaw, and springs clear. Curly rushes again. The dog knocks her down.

(SOUND: a metallic clip of teeth; thirty huskies snarling and yelping)

The pack closes over Curly. Francois swings an axe and scatters them, but Curly lies lifeless in the trampled snow. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue as if laughing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Buck would see to it that he never went down.

Francois fastens a harness over Buck's long coat and sets him between Dave and Sol-leks, a long, gaunt, battle-scarred husky with one flashing eye.

(SOUND: leather traces tightening; Francois's whip cracking once)

Buck hauls a sledload of firewood, awkward at first, then straining eagerly beside the experienced dogs.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

EXT. DYEA TRAIL CAMP - NIGHT

A candlelit tent glows in the frozen camp. When Buck enters, Perrault and Francois drive him out with curses and cooking utensils.

(SOUND: a bitter wind rattling tent canvas; pots clanging)

Buck shivers on the snow until his forelegs break through a drift. Beneath it, BILLEE, a gentle husky, lies curled in a warm hollow and licks Buck's face.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another lesson. So that was the way they did it.

Buck paws a snow hole beside Billee, circles into it, and falls asleep. At gray dawn he bursts up through fresh snow, snarling at the buried darkness.

(SOUND: campfires catching, dogs barking, harness buckles clinking)

PERRAULT
Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

The nine-dog team swings onto the trail.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck runs in harness behind Spitz, with Dave and Sol-leks teaching him by nipping whenever he tangles the traces.

(SOUND: runners scraping hard snow; the steady pant of working dogs)

Days pass in hard travel. Buck learns “ho,” “mush,” and the sharp turns of a loaded sled. At camp, he gulps dried salmon before the other dogs can steal it.

Buck watches PIKE take bacon while Perrault's back is turned. The next day Buck steals the whole chunk; DUB is punished for it.

(SOUND: Perrault's angry shout, a whip snapping, hungry dogs snarling over fish)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the Northland, under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Driving snow blinds the camp on the frozen lake. Buck digs a snug nest beneath a rock wall, eats his fish, and returns to find Spitz occupying it.

(SOUND: ice groaning beneath the campfire; wind cutting across the lake)

Buck springs on Spitz. The white dog circles, crying with rage.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

A club strikes outside the firelight. Starving huskies pour into camp, overturning the grub-box and tearing through bread, bacon, lashings, and leather.

(SOUND: clubs pounding ribs, frantic yelps, the crash of the overturned grub-box)

Buck fights three invaders, rips one at the throat, then feels teeth slash into his own neck. Spitz has attacked from the side.

Buck braces against Spitz's charge and escapes with the team into the forest.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit streaks along the frozen creek, pursued by Buck and a pack of sixty dogs under moonlight.

(SOUND: the pack's rising wolf-cry and the rabbit's frantic feet on crusted snow)

Spitz cuts across a bend and springs into the rabbit's path. His teeth break its back in midair.

(SOUND: the rabbit's piercing shriek; the pack answering in a hellish chorus)

Buck drives shoulder-first into Spitz. They roll through powdery snow, then rise to circle, their breaths silver in the frozen air.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The time had come. It was to the death.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and leaps away. Buck feints for the throat, sweeps low, and crunches Spitz's left foreleg. He repeats the trick and breaks the right.

(SOUND: fangs snapping like steel traps; Buck's labored panting)

The silent ring of dogs tightens around the crippled leader. Buck makes the final rush. Spitz disappears beneath the dark circle.

(SOUND: snarling surges, then falls abruptly still)

Buck stands alone on the moon-flooded snow--the dominant primordial beast who has made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

At dawn, a scarred Buck stands before the harness line. Francois brings Sol-leks forward to fill Spitz's place, but Buck drives the one-eyed dog aside and takes the lead.

(SOUND: impatient dogs whining; harness chains twitching)

FRANCOIS
Go 'way, Chook!

Francois advances with a club. Buck retreats just beyond its reach, snarling but refusing to leave the lead position.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots at once into the front traces.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership.

Buck snaps Pike back into his breast-band, disciplines Joe, and pulls the team into one united line. The sled flies over packed snow toward Skaguay.

(SOUND: runners hissing fast over snow; bells jingling in a quick rhythm)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

At Skaguay, Perrault and Francois embrace Buck, then disappear into the crowd.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Weeks of mail hauling have drained the team. Buck's once-glossy long coat is dull with frost, but his brown muzzle and white chest remain at the head of the traces.

(SOUND: tired bells, dragging runners, Dave's painful cry)

DAVE repeatedly falls. A MAIL DRIVER removes him from the harness, but Dave bites through Sol-leks's traces and stands proudly in his old place.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The pride of trace and trail was his.

Dave pulls until camp. The next morning he can only worm himself through the snow toward the harnesses.

(SOUND: Dave's mournful howling behind the departing team)

The mail driver walks back through the river timber. A revolver shot rings out.

(SOUND: one distant revolver shot, followed by the faint jingle of the moving team)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. SKAGUAY DOG CAMP - DAY

Buck and the surviving mail dogs stand footsore and gaunt beside a slipshod camp. HAL, a callow young man with a revolver belt and hunting knife, argues with CHARLES, whose weak watery eyes peer beneath a fierce mustache. MERCEDES, pretty and soft in a heavy coat, fusses beside them.

(SOUND: Skaguay traffic, dog teams passing, tin dishes clattering)

Charles and Hal pile blankets, dishes, tents, and clothes onto a top-heavy sled. TOM, a seasoned musher, watches from a neighboring camp.

TOM
You've got a right smart load as it is. The runners are froze fast. Throw your weight against the gee-pole, right and left, and break it out.

HAL
Mush on there!

The dogs throw themselves against the breast-bands. The sled does not move. Hal raises his whip; Mercedes seizes his arm.

MERCEDES
You poor, poor dears. Why don't you pull hard? Then you wouldn't be whipped.

Hal breaks the runners loose. The overloaded sled lurches ahead, overturns on the first turn, and scatters supplies down Skaguay's main street.

(SOUND: whips cracking, sled runners grinding, cookware smashing across packed snow)

The three travelers buy six Outside dogs and depart with fourteen animals in the traces.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Spring sun melts the trail from above while water eats at the river ice below. Buck, now a skeletal long-haired giant, staggers under loose hide; every rib shows beneath his white chest blaze.

(SOUND: meltwater trickling, ice fissures popping, exhausted dogs wheezing)

The Outside dogs die first. Dub and Billee vanish from the traces. The remaining dogs gnaw strips of frozen horsehide while Charles, Hal, and Mercedes quarrel over the unbuilt fire and the failing load.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was not food that Buck and the huskies needed, but rest.

Hal's club drops Buck, then drives him back to his feet. Joe, Pike, Sol-leks, and Teek stumble behind him, nearly dead.

(SOUND: Hal's club thudding; a sharp crack racing under the ice)

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Ahead, the mouth of White River opens beneath rotting ice.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON, a lean man still limping slightly from frozen feet, sits by a birch fire whittling an axe handle. Buck's ruined team collapses at the edge of his camp.

(SOUND: axe blade shaving wood; spring water rushing beneath river ice)

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

THORNTON
And they told you true. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

Hal lashes the dogs. Sol-leks, Teek, Joe, and Pike painfully rise. Buck lies still.

(SOUND: the lash whistling and striking Buck's body)

Hal exchanges the whip for a club. Thornton rises, his face tightening.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way. I'm going to Dawson.

Hal draws his knife. Thornton knocks it from his hand with the axe handle, cuts Buck free from the traces, and stands over him.

(SOUND: the knife clattering on ice; Mercedes screaming)

Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the remaining dogs move onto the river. A quarter mile out, the trail drops away beneath them.

(SOUND: a vast sheet of ice cracking; a sled plunging into black water)

The sled, humans, and dogs vanish. Thornton kneels beside Buck, who raises his head and licks his hand.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Days later, Buck's long fur has regained its sheen, his broad chest has filled out, and the brown muzzle and white chest blaze are clean again. SKEET, a little Irish setter, tenderly cleans his wounds while NIG, a huge black deerhound-bloodhound, watches with laughing eyes.

(SOUND: birds calling, firewood snapping, river water running free)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

Thornton takes Buck's head between his hands, presses his own head against it, and shakes him roughly with affection. Buck catches Thornton's hand in his mouth and closes his teeth without breaking skin.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck lies at Thornton's feet, watching his every movement. Beyond the campfire, the forest deepens into green shadow.

(SOUND: a distant wolf howl carried through the trees)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Deep in the forest a call was sounding.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

By autumn, Buck is a powerful one-hundred-and-fifty-pound dog, long-coated and broad-shouldered, with his brown muzzle and white chest bright against the spray. He races the bank beside Thornton's poling boat while HANS and PETE handle a Manila rope from tree to tree.

(SOUND: rapids roaring against submerged rocks; rope rasping through gloved hands)

The boat catches on a ledge, flips, and throws Thornton into the racing current. Buck leaps in and reaches him.

THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Thornton clings to Buck's tail, but the current drives them toward the worst water. Thornton catches a rock and releases him. Buck swims obediently to shore.

Hans and Pete strap the rope around Buck's neck and shoulders, then launch him again. He misses Thornton and is jerked under by the tightening line.

(SOUND: Buck dragged beneath the water; Hans and Pete pounding breath back into him)

Thornton's distant voice reaches Buck. He springs up. On the second launch, he holds the current, turns precisely above Thornton, and slams into him like a battering ram.

(SOUND: the rope snapping taut around a tree; bodies smashing through rapids)

Hans and Pete haul Buck and Thornton onto the bank. Thornton crawls to Buck's side and finds three broken ribs.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON STREET - DAY

Outside the Eldorado Saloon, a sled loaded with twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour stands frozen to the hard-packed snow. Fur-clad miners form a ring around Buck and Thornton.

(SOUND: gamblers murmuring, boots crunching, saloon piano faint behind the doors)

THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? And walk off with it for a hundred yards?

THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JIM O'BRIEN places his sack of gold beside Matthewson's.

O'BRIEN
Sure. Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

Hans and Pete add every dollar they possess. The ten-dog team is unhitched. Buck alone is fastened to the sled.

Thornton kneels and presses cheek to cheek with him.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck takes Thornton's mittened hand between his teeth, presses, and slowly releases it.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings right, taking up slack. The runners crack loose.

(SOUND: ice snapping beneath the runners; the crowd falling silent)

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. The sled pivots free.

THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws his whole body forward. His claws score parallel grooves in the snow. The sled jerks--then moves steadily past the marker.

(SOUND: a thunderous cheer, hats and mittens flying)

Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, cursing him softly and lovingly as tears run down his face.

EXT. CREEK GORGE - NIGHT

Under a summer midnight sky, a long, lean timber wolf howls in an open space beside a running creek. Buck steps from the trees, his mane lifted in waves.

(SOUND: the wolf's long-drawn howl echoed by the creek)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The call was many-noted, sounding more luringly and compellingly than ever before.

The wolf flees. Buck pursues him through the gorge until the wolf is cornered against a timber jam. Buck does not attack. He circles with friendly advances.

(SOUND: cautious snarls fading into soft whines)

At last wolf and dog touch noses. They run side by side over the divide until they stop at a stream.

Buck remembers Thornton. He turns toward camp. The wolf follows for a while, then sits, points his nose to the sky, and gives a mournful howl.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

A spruce-bough lodge stands in a broad valley beside a shallow placer. Moose-hide sacks of gold dust and nuggets are piled outside like firewood. Thornton, lean and weathered, works with Hans and Pete beside the sluice boxes.

(SOUND: pickaxes striking gravel; water splashing through wooden sluices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When Buck earned sixteen hundred dollars in five minutes, he made it possible for his master to pay his debts and journey into the East after a fabled lost mine.

Days later, Buck returns from the forest, huge and splendid in his long coat, but he stops at a fresh human trail. The woods ahead are unnaturally silent.

(SOUND: no birds--only Buck's careful breathing through the brush)

Buck finds Nig dead in a thicket, an arrow through his body. Farther on, HANS lies facedown, feathered with arrows. In the clearing, YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wreckage of the lodge.

(SOUND: Yeehat voices rising in a sing-song chant; arrows rattling in quivers)

Buck roars and charges. He tears the Yeehat chief's throat, strikes another hunter in passing, and drives through the panic before arrows can find him.

(SOUND: Buck's savage roar, men shouting, arrows striking trees and flesh)

The hunters flee into the timber. Buck returns to the ruined camp. PETE lies dead in his blankets. Skeet lies at the muddy pool's edge, her forepaws in the water.

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the pool. No scent leads out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He knew John Thornton was dead.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Full moonlight bathes the ruined camp and the muddy pool. Buck stands alone beside the water, motionless beneath the northern sky.

(SOUND: wind moving through spruce branches; one low, grieving whine)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

From the forest comes a chorus of sharp yelps. A wolf pack pours into the clearing. Buck breaks the neck of the first wolf to leap at him, then backs into a gravel-bank angle and faces the pack.

(SOUND: wolves growling, fangs snapping, paws tearing at gravel)

After the pack draws back, the long, lean wolf from the creek approaches. Buck recognizes his wild brother. They touch noses.

An old, battle-scarred wolf raises his muzzle to the moon and howls. The pack joins him. Buck sits, lifts his own muzzle, and answers.

(SOUND: Buck's wolf howl joining the pack; a low musical swell beneath it)

The wolves spring into the forest. Buck runs with them, side by side with his wild brother, then takes the lead beneath the pale borealis.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His great throat a-bellow, he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.
